# Line endings.
#
# Git on Windows converts to CRLF on checkout by default. For most files that
# is harmless, but a few are read by Linux inside a container, where a stray
# carriage return is not whitespace:
#
#   - a shell script with CRLF fails as "bad interpreter: /bin/sh^M"
#   - an LDIF with CRLF imports as empty, which looks exactly like a wrong
#     base DN and costs an afternoon to find
#
# So those are pinned to LF regardless of platform or user configuration.

* text=auto

*.sh        text eol=lf
*.ldif      text eol=lf
*.conf      text eol=lf
Dockerfile* text eol=lf
*.yml       text eol=lf
*.yaml      text eol=lf

# Go tooling writes and expects LF everywhere.
*.go        text eol=lf
go.mod      text eol=lf
go.sum      text eol=lf

# The embedded web assets are served byte for byte; CRLF would only inflate
# them and make diffs noisier.
*.js        text eol=lf
*.css       text eol=lf
*.html      text eol=lf
*.json      text eol=lf
*.md        text eol=lf
